---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import { GAME_IMAGES } from '../../lib/images';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Conveyor belt fix', path: '/guides/first-station-logistics/' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'power', label: 'Power the station' },
  { id: 'research', label: 'Queue Logistics I' },
  { id: 'belts', label: 'Build the first belt' },
  { id: 'troubleshooting', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
  { id: 'context', label: 'What is confirmed' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'Belt questions' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Why are conveyor belts missing in Main Sequence?',
    answer:
      'In the current demo, conveyor belts are tied to the Logistics I research step. They are not a starting build option. A demo player report confirms that researching Logistics I makes belt construction available.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why won’t my research station start?',
    answer:
      'According to a demo player report, station machines need station-side power. Building a generator on the station and supplying it with power items was the solution in that playthrough. A docked ship did not share power with the station. This is community-tested rather than an official tutorial line.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much research does Logistics I need?',
    answer:
      'The official demo screenshot shows Logistics I in the Research Queue at 0/10. We do not treat that as a permanent cost because the demo is still being patched.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I skip logistics and keep hand-feeding machines?',
    answer:
      'You can hand-feed a small amount of production, but the game is a factory builder and belts are a core automation tool. If your goal is to see the demo’s production loop, unlocking Logistics I is the practical path.',
  },
];

const block = 'scroll-mt-28';
---

<GuideLayout
  seoTitle="Main Sequence Conveyor Belts: How to Unlock Logistics I"
  description="Conveyor belts missing in Main Sequence? Power your station, research Logistics I and build your first belt line with this demo-focused step-by-step guide."
  eyebrow="Demo field fix"
  title="Why conveyor belts are missing in Main Sequence — and how to unlock them"
  intro="Belts are not bugged. In the current demo they sit behind a station-power and Logistics I research step that the tutorial does not explain clearly."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  sourceIds={[
    'steam-forum-logistics',
    'steam-screens',
    'steam-news-2026-09-21',
    'steam-news-2026-10-21',
    'steam-news-2025-08-12',
    'steam-news-2026-05-28',
  ]}
  image={GAME_IMAGES['main-sequence-conveyor-belt-build.jpg']}
  imageAlt="A blue holographic Conveyor Belt Mk. 1 being placed inside a Main Sequence station"
  imageCaption="The conveyor build screen in the current demo. Belt availability is tied to progression, not just the hotbar."
  meta={['Demo: October 21 build', 'Community-tested step labelled', 'Spoiler-light']}
  faq={faq}
  pageType="WebPage"
  dataNote="The specific power and Logistics I sequence comes from a player-tested demo thread. Validate it against your current patch; the underlying research gate is visible in official media."
>
  <section id="answer" class={block}>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-title">Quick answer</span>
      <p class="m-0">
        Build a powered station first. Then use the station’s research/science
        machine to queue <strong>Logistics I</strong>. Once that research is
        complete, conveyor belt construction becomes available. In the current
        demo, the station’s power comes from station-side generation; a docked
        ship does not solve it, according to a community-tested report.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The confusion is understandable. The mission text tells you to connect
      machines with a conveyor belt, but does not expose belts in the build
      menu. The official demo screenshot shows Logistics I in a research queue
      at 0/10, which supports what players found: this is a progression gate,
      not a missing item.
    </p>
    <p>
      The following route is split into the part that is visible in official
      material and the part that comes from player testing. It reflects the
      October 21, 2026 demo hotfix.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="power" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 1: Give the station power</h2>
    <p>
      Place station-side power generation before you queue research. In the
      community thread that documented this blocker, the working sequence was:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Build a generator on the station.</li>
      <li>Supply it with the power/fuel item it accepts.</li>
      <li>Place the research machine on the same powered station.</li>
      <li>Feed the research machine the input it requests.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The player who reported the issue had a powered ship docked at the
      station, but the research machine still had no power. That suggests the
      docked ship and station do not share a power pool in the current demo.
      Treat this as a player-tested limitation, not a permanent design promise.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-warning">
      <span class="callout-title">Status: community-tested</span>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm">
        The official tutorial does not clearly list the station-power step. The
        exact generator, fuel item and build menu labels may have changed since
        the report. Check the machine’s configuration panel for its power
        requirements.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>What to check before you build research</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The generator is actually on the station, not only on the docked ship.</li>
      <li>The generator has a fuel or power input connected.</li>
      <li>The research machine is powered and is not showing a red power state.</li>
      <li>The science/research input is present in storage or the machine’s input.</li>
      <li>The station itself is powered rather than only being physically built.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The developer describes station power as a real constraint: mining drone
      bays and machines require enough power to run. The specific solution in
      the current demo is the part learned from players.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="research" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 2: Queue and finish Logistics I</h2>
    <p>
      Open the station’s research interface. The official research screenshot
      shows a queue entry for <strong>Logistics I</strong> with a 0/10 progress
      indicator and an “Unlocks” panel beside it. The same screenshot shows the
      Automation branch continuing to Mining Drones, Computation Science and
      Logistics II.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Interact with the research/science module on the powered station.</li>
      <li>Add Logistics I to the research queue.</li>
      <li>Supply the research machine with the correct research item.</li>
      <li>Wait for the queue to complete; check that station power stays stable.</li>
      <li>Return to the build menu and look for the conveyor build option.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Do not assume that “research complete” means “all belts unlocked forever.”
      Later logistics technology may add more options. For the first belt, the
      current demo progression is Logistics I.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="belts" class={block}>
    <h2>Step 3: Build the first belt without making a mess</h2>
    <p>
      The official build screenshot identifies the tool as <strong>Conveyor
      Belt Mk. 1</strong>. Its on-screen build controls show:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Confirm All</strong> for the selected line.</li>
      <li><strong>Add Segment</strong> to continue the route.</li>
      <li><strong>Rotate</strong> to change direction.</li>
      <li><strong>Cancel Segment</strong> to undo the current segment.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Start with a short, straight connection between one output and one
      storage or machine input. Do not build a decorative loop before you know
      the item can travel the route.
    </p>

    <h3>A reliable first belt test</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Place a machine with a completed recipe.</li>
      <li>Place a container or the next machine directly beside it.</li>
      <li>Connect the output to the input with a short belt.</li>
      <li>Supply the source manually and watch one item move.</li>
      <li>Only then extend the line or add a splitter.</li>
    </ol>

    <Figure
      name="main-sequence-production-lines.jpg"
      alt="A Main Sequence production room with machines in rows and conveyor belts running between them"
      caption="Keep the first belt short enough that you can see both ends. Expand after the item moves reliably."
      sizes="(min-width: 768px) 760px, 100vw"
      class="my-6"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class={block}>
    <h2>Troubleshooting checklist</h2>
    <div class="data-table-wrap not-prose mt-5">
      <table class="data-table data-table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Symptom</th>
            <th scope="col">Check first</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Symptom">No conveyor in the build menu</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Check first">Logistics I research complete, station is powered, current demo build installed.</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Community-tested / visible gate</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Symptom">Research never progresses</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Check first">Station generator running, research machine powered, correct research item supplied.</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Community-tested</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Symptom">Belt exists but items do not move</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Check first">Machine output enabled, destination input accepts the item, belt direction is correct.</td>
            <td data-label="Status">General UI check</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Symptom">The tutorial contradicts this</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Check first">Check the October 21 hotfix notes and the in-game Codex. Tutorial gaps are a known demo complaint.</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Community report</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Symptom">Research option seems absent</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Check first">Check prerequisites and current demo version. Players have reported missing tutorial steps; we do not have an official fix list for every case.</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Unresolved / update-dependent</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-mist">
      If your build differs from the October 21 demo, trust the in-game
      configuration panels over an older guide. The game is still changing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="context" class={block}>
    <h2>What is actually confirmed?</h2>
    <p>
      There is a difference between the official information and the
      player-tested route that solves the blocker:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Official:</strong> conveyor belts are part of station logistics;
        the developer describes connecting machines with belts. The official
        screenshot shows Logistics I in the research queue and a Conveyor Belt
        Mk. 1 build tool.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Player-tested:</strong> station machines need station-side power,
        a docked ship did not supply that power in one reported playthrough, and
        belts became available after Logistics I was researched.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unknown:</strong> whether the exact sequence will remain
        unchanged at Early Access, and whether the tutorial will explain it more
        clearly.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We keep the player-tested part visible because it solves a real problem,
      but we do not dress it up as an official design document. For the broader
      opening, return to the <a href="/beginner-guide/">beginner’s guide</a>.
      For the technology branches, use the{' '}
      <a href="/database/research/">research database</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
